Z||000002||dogtired||11-16-1999||08:51 AM||||OK remember you asked for it. Not sure I would do this one in the kitchen?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>This one was told about on the Discovery Channel. If you are out in the desert and you loose all the electrolite (acid) from your battery you can urinate in your battery. You notice I said YOUR battery - stay away from mine. Anyway uric acid (urine) has some of the same characteristics of electrolite (battery acid) and will give a battery enough charge to start the vehicle? Now don't forget, YOU asked.<BR>I wouldn't stand to close   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">  that would sure hurt in the morning!<BR>If you test thes theory, I really don't care to know the result.||||reg||12
Z||000003||Raydar||11-16-1999||11:02 AM||||If you do this, make sure that you are not touching anything metal, on the car. Salty water is an *excellent* conductor of electricity. You *don't* want to complete a circuit. I don't even wanna think about it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RICK||11-16-1999||11:21 AM||||Kel...Well, nevermind, I could get in trouble. LOL||||reg||10
Z||000005||batboy||11-16-1999||03:25 PM||||Rick, I think you're already in trouble!<P>Here's a couple stories that happen to me will four wheeling and caving in the vast wilderness of the Guadalupe Mountains of New Mexico. I was inching my way up a rugged mountain side in my Suburban and cut a tire on a rock. Slapped the spare on and 10 minutes later, KABOOM! Ripped the whole side wall out of the brand new spare that I just put on.<P>Took the first flat tire back out, crammed three tire patching plugs and a broken pencil into the gaping hole in the tread, then pumped it back up with my portable air pump that you plug into the cigarette light. We had to stop several times to keep adding air, but made back to civilization.<P>Back up in the mountains again a couple days later (with two new tires), the other vehicle that was with us hit a rock and punctured his gas tank. He used a bar of Ivory soap to seal up the leak until he got back home.<P>Another story, I had a friend years ago who had a tie rod pop loose on his late '40s Chevy truck. He wired it back on and limped on home.<P>Same friend was working under a car when the "bottle" hydraulic jack slipped out, pinning him under the car. He wasn't injuried too bad, but was in a fair amount of pain with the car frame compressing down on his chest, not to mention making it extremely hard to breathe. Nobody heard his cries for help, so blindly felt around until he latched onto a wrench. Using the wrench he fished a long ratchet extension within reach. Straining and after many attempts, he used the extention to wiggle the jack within reach and finally got the car jacked up high enough to crawl out. He figures that he must of been pinned there for at least 20 minutes, but it seemed like a decade.||||reg||1
Z||000006||dogtired||11-16-1999||03:56 PM||||Here Hold this battery for me whill I - Never Mind!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> <P>While talking of batteries. How about taking your terminal ends off your battery and soaking them in Coca-Cola over night. It will clean the corrosion off. ( normal battery corrosion, not the residue from above)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Who started this anyway??||||reg||6
Z||000007||Phil||11-16-1999||04:20 PM||||must have been started by some ham & egger||||reg||1
